List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");

// ═══ toArray() — returns Object[] ═══
Object[] objArr = accounts.toArray();
// objArr is Object[], NOT String[]!
// (String[]) objArr → ClassCastException! Object[] ≠ String[]!

// ═══ toArray(T[]) — returns typed array ═══
String[] strArr = accounts.toArray(new String[0]);
// Passes an empty String[] as "type hint"
// JVM creates a new String[] of correct size → returns String[]!
// new String[0] is preferred over new String[list.size()]:
// JVM optimizes new String[0] better (JIT creates right-sized array directly)

// ═══ toArray(IntFunction) — Java 11+ cleanest ═══
String[] strArr2 = accounts.toArray(String[]::new);
// Method reference: String[]::new is equivalent to size -> new String[size]

// ═══ WHY new String[0] instead of new String[list.size()]? ═══
// Counterintuitively, new String[0] is FASTER in modern JVMs:
// With new String[0]: JVM creates a perfectly-sized array internally (JIT optimized)
// With new String[size]: JVM creates the array, THEN copies — one extra step
// Benchmark: new String[0] is ~10% faster (JMH verified)
